          %h2#introduction.num_section Introduction
          %p
            A
            %em kinematic tangent space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            at some point is an equivalence class of smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
            through that point, regraded as equivalent if their first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            coincide. This generalizes the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operational+tangent+space"} operational tangent space
            which for differentiable manifolds coincides, but more generally need not.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} Generalised smooth spaces
            are, as the name suggests, generalisations of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Spivak79"} Spivak 79
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
            the various notions are no longer equivalent.
          %p
            One of the more intuitive definitions of the tangent space of a manifold is as the space of derivatives of smooth curves. Roughly speaking, in this view the tangent space of a manifold at a point is the home of all derivatives of smooth curves through that point. To differentiate this notion from the other definitions, it is sometimes called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong kinematic tangent space
          %p
            Once we go beyond ordinary smooth manifolds, the kinematic tangent space of a smooth space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may not have some or all of its “usual” properties. In particular, it may not even be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is also called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlinear+space"} microlinear space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and possibly under some more conditions. ) However, it will always be a kind of
            %em partial
            vector space.
          %p
            It is also possible to define “higher” kinematic tangent spaces. This is not done by iterating the construction, but rather by considering a slightly different construction: instead of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            of the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1)
            is just
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ 0\}
            because any curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha (0) = 0
            must also have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha '(0) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
